<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter12_structured-text-records-and-csv">
    <title>Structured Text Records and CSV Parsing</title>

    <p>
        A <term>record</term> is one complete logical item of data in a file. In
        a comma-separated score file, each line is one record containing a name
        and a score. A file can contain many records, while a C++ structure can
        represent one record in memory.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing_chapter12_score-csv-input">
        <title>Example file: <c>scores.csv</c></title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_score-csv-input"
                 language="text" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
Amina,92
Marco,85
Li,97
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
        A simple CSV parser can treat the comma as a delimiter. This example
        intentionally supports simple records only: a name cannot contain an
        unquoted comma. Full CSV formats also support quoted fields, embedded
        commas, and escaped quotation marks.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing_chapter12_parse-csv-record">
        <title>Parsing and validating one CSV record</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_parse-csv-record"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

struct ScoreRecord {
    std::string name;
    int score{};
};

bool parse_score_record(const std::string& line, ScoreRecord& record) {
    std::istringstream record_stream{line};
    std::string name;
    int score{};

    if (!std::getline(record_stream, name, ',')) {
        return false;
    }

    if (!(record_stream >> score)) {
        return false;
    }

    record_stream >> std::ws;
    if (!record_stream.eof()) {
        return false;
    }

    if (name.empty() || score < 0 || score > 100) {
        return false;
    }

    record = ScoreRecord{name, score};
    return true;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
        The parser first separates the fields, then converts the score, rejects
        extra non-whitespace data, checks the score range, and finally updates
        the output object. Delaying the assignment helps prevent a partially
        parsed record from being mistaken for a valid one.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing_chapter12_read-csv-file">
        <title>Reading and validating a CSV file</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_read-csv-file"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <sstream>
#include <string>

struct ScoreRecord {
    std::string name;
    int score{};
};

bool parse_score_record(const std::string& line, ScoreRecord& record) {
    std::istringstream record_stream{line};
    std::string name;
    int score{};

    if (!std::getline(record_stream, name, ',')) {
        return false;
    }
    if (!(record_stream >> score)) {
        return false;
    }

    record_stream >> std::ws;
    if (!record_stream.eof() || name.empty() || score < 0 || score > 100) {
        return false;
    }

    record = ScoreRecord{name, score};
    return true;
}

int main() {
    std::ifstream input_file{"scores.csv"};

    if (!input_file) {
        return 1;
    }

    std::string line;
    ScoreRecord record;

    while (std::getline(input_file, line)) {
        if (parse_score_record(line, record)) {
            std::cout << record.name << ": "
                      << record.score << '\n';
        }
        else {
            std::cerr << "Skipping malformed record: "
                      << line << '\n';
        }
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
        The program opens <c>scores.csv</c>, reads one complete line at a time,
        parses each line, and processes only valid records. The parser does not
        ask for keyboard input; it receives a line as a parameter, which keeps
        file processing and user interaction separate.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing_chapter12_write-csv-file">
        <title>Writing records to a CSV file</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_write-csv-file"
                 language="cpp" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>
#include <string>
#include <vector>

struct ScoreRecord {
    std::string name;
    int score{};
};

int main() {
    std::vector<ScoreRecord> records{
        {"Amina", 92},
        {"Marco", 85},
        {"Li", 97}
    };

    std::ofstream output_file{"scores.csv"};

    if (!output_file) {
        return 1;
    }

    for (const ScoreRecord& record : records) {
        output_file << record.name << ','
                    << record.score << '\n';
    }

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
        By default, opening an <c>std::ofstream</c> for writing into a file
		creates the file if the file does not exist. If the file already exists,
        C++ overwrites the existing file causing the loss of original data.
        This behavior is useful when the program is meant to create a fresh
        output file, but it would destroy earlier records such as in a growing log file.
    </p>

    <p>
        To preserve the existing contents and add new records at the end, open
        the stream with <c>std::ios::app</c>, where <c>app</c> means
        <q>append</q>. Each output operation then writes at the end of the file.
        If the file does not yet exist, append mode creates it.
    </p>

    <listing xml:id="listing_chapter12_append-csv-file">
        <title>Appending a record without erasing earlier records</title>
        <program xml:id="program_chapter12_append-csv-file"
                 language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <fstream>

int main() {
    std::ofstream output_file{
        "scores.csv",
        std::ios::app
    };

    if (!output_file) {
        return 1;
    }

    output_file << "Noor,88\n";
    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
        </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
        Running this program again adds another <c>Noor,88</c> record rather than
        replacing the records already in <c>scores.csv</c>. A program that uses
        append mode should therefore decide whether duplicate records are
        acceptable. Both the regular writing example and this append example
        assume that names do not contain unquoted commas; a full CSV
        implementation must handle quoted fields.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter12_tsv-and-json-preview">
        <title>Other structured text formats</title>
        <p>
            A TSV file uses tab characters instead of commas and can be handled
            with a similar strategy. JSON is another popular structured format,
            but C++17 has no JSON library in the standard library. A production
            program normally uses a carefully selected JSON library rather than
            writing a complete JSON parser as a beginner exercise.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <exercise label="chapter12_3_record-definition">
        <statement><p>What is a record in a structured text file?</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes">
            <choice><statement><p>A second file created during parsing</p></statement><feedback><p>A record is data within a file, not another file.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>One complete logical item of data</p></statement><feedback><p>For this example, each CSV line is one score record.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Only a pointer to a file</p></statement><feedback><p>A pointer is not a logical data record.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>A compiler error message</p></statement><feedback><p>Errors are not records in the CSV format.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>The entire computer memory</p></statement><feedback><p>A record is a bounded logical item.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_3_validation">
        <statement><p>Select all reasons to validate a parsed record.</p></statement>
        <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>A field may be missing or have the wrong type.</p></statement><feedback><p>Input can be structurally malformed.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice correct="yes"><statement><p>A value may have the right type but violate an application rule.</p></statement><feedback><p>An integer score can still be outside the allowed range.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Text input is always valid once it is read.</p></statement><feedback><p>Successful reading does not guarantee meaningful data.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>Validation is needed only for binary files.</p></statement><feedback><p>Text records also require validation.</p></feedback></choice>
            <choice><statement><p>A parser should silently guess when the format is unclear.</p></statement><feedback><p>Documented formats and explicit failure are safer.</p></feedback></choice>
        </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise label="chapter12_3_csv-code" number="yes" adaptive="yes" indentation="show">
        <statement><p>Arrange the blocks to parse the name and score from one CSV line.</p></statement>
        <blocks>
            <block order="1"><cline>std::istringstream record_stream{line};</cline></block>
            <block order="2"><cline>std::getline(record_stream, name, ',');</cline></block>
            <block order="3"><cline>record_stream &gt;&gt; score;</cline></block>
            <block order="4"><cline>record_stream &gt;&gt; std::ws;</cline></block>
            <block order="5"><cline>return !name.empty() &amp;&amp; record_stream.eof();</cline></block>
        </blocks>
    </exercise>
</section>
